    "value": "large (little cytoplasm)",
    "quote": "The two basic types of lymphocytes, B cells and T cells, are identical morphologically with a large central nucleus surrounded by a thin layer of cytoplasm.",
    "source": {
     "section": "21.1 Anatomy of the Lymphatic and Immune Systems",
     "url": "https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/21-1-anatomy-of-the-lymphatic-and-immune-systems",
     "license": "CC BY 4.0",
     "citation": "Betts JG, Young KA, Wise JA, Johnson E, Poe B, Kruse DH, Korol O, Johnson JE, Womble M, DeSaix P (2022). Anatomy and Physiology 2e. OpenStax.",
     "access": "Access for free at https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/1-introduction"
    }
   },
   {
    "field": "special",
    "value": "B cell receptors: thousands of membrane-bound immunoglobulin (antibody) molecules coating the surface of the naive cell",
    "quote": "Similar to T cells, naïve B cells initially are coated in thousands of B cell receptors (BCRs), which are membrane-bound forms of Ig (immunoglobulin, or an antibody).",
    "source": {
     "section": "42.2 Adaptive Immune Response",
     "url": "https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/42-2-adaptive-immune-response",
     "license": "CC BY 4.0",
     "citation": "Clark MA, Douglas M, Choi J (2018). Biology 2e. OpenStax.",
     "access": "Access for free at https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/1-introduction"
    }
   },
   {
    "field": "special",
    "value": "B cell receptor built from two heavy chains and two light chains joined by disulfide linkages",
    "quote": "The B cell receptor has two heavy chains and two light chains connected by disulfide linkages.",
    "source": {
     "section": "42.2 Adaptive Immune Response",
     "url": "https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/42-2-adaptive-immune-response",
     "license": "CC BY 4.0",
     "citation": "Clark MA, Douglas M, Choi J (2018). Biology 2e. OpenStax.",
     "access": "Access for free at https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/1-introduction"
    }
   },
   {
    "field": "special",
    "value": "surface IgM and IgD (both receptor classes on mature B cells leaving the bone marrow, with the same antigen specificity)",
    "quote": "Mature B cells that leave the bone marrow express both IgM and IgD, but both antibodies have the same antigen specificity.",
    "source": {
     "section": "21.4 The Adaptive Immune Response: B-lymphocytes and Antibodies",
     "url": "https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/21-4-the-adaptive-immune-response-b-lymphocytes-and-antibodies",
     "license": "CC BY 4.0",
     "citation": "Betts JG, Young KA, Wise JA, Johnson E, Poe B, Kruse DH, Korol O, Johnson JE, Womble M, DeSaix P (2022). Anatomy and Physiology 2e. OpenStax.",
     "access": "Access for free at https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/1-introduction"
    }
   },
   {
    "field": "special",
    "value": "Ig alpha and Ig beta: two further membrane proteins, involved in signaling",
    "quote": "Two other membrane proteins, Ig alpha and Ig beta, are involved in signaling.",
    "source": {
     "section": "42.2 Adaptive Immune Response",
     "url": "https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/42-2-adaptive-immune-response",
     "license": "CC BY 4.0",
     "citation": "Clark MA, Douglas M, Choi J (2018). Biology 2e. OpenStax.",
     "access": "Access for free at https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/1-introduction"
    }
   },
   {
    "field": "special",
    "value": "MHC class II molecules that display processed antigen to Th2 helper T cells",
    "quote": "They bind and engulf foreign antigens via their BCRs and then display processed antigens in the context of MHC II molecules to TH2 cells.",
    "source": {
     "section": "42.2 Adaptive Immune Response",
     "url": "https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/42-2-adaptive-immune-response",
     "license": "CC BY 4.0",
     "citation": "Clark MA, Douglas M, Choi J (2018). Biology 2e. OpenStax.",
     "access": "Access for free at https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/1-introduction"
    }
   },
   {
    "field": "function",
    "value": "Unlike a T cell, a B cell recognizes native, unprocessed antigen without needing MHC molecules or antigen-presenting cells.",
    "quote": "B cells do not recognize antigen in the complex fashion of T cells. B cells can recognize native, unprocessed antigen and do not require the participation of MHC molecules and antigen-presenting cells.",
    "source": {
     "section": "21.4 The Adaptive Immune Response: B-lymphocytes and Antibodies",
     "url": "https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/21-4-the-adaptive-immune-response-b-lymphocytes-and-antibodies",
     "license": "CC BY 4.0",
     "citation": "Betts JG, Young KA, Wise JA, Johnson E, Poe B, Kruse DH, Korol O, Johnson JE, Womble M, DeSaix P (2022). Anatomy and Physiology 2e. OpenStax.",
     "access": "Access for free at https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/1-introduction"
    }
   },
   {
    "field": "function",
    "value": "A B cell binds intact antigen because the antibodies it secretes must recognize the pathogen itself rather than digested remnants of it.",
    "quote": "B cells must be able to bind intact antigens because they secrete antibodies that must recognize the pathogen directly, rather than digested remnants of the pathogen.",
    "source": {
     "section": "42.2 Adaptive Immune Response",
     "url": "https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/42-2-adaptive-immune-response",
     "license": "CC BY 4.0",
     "citation": "Clark MA, Douglas M, Choi J (2018). Biology 2e. OpenStax.",
     "access": "Access for free at https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/1-introduction"
    }
   },
   {
    "field": "function",
    "value": "Each surface antibody has two binding sites, so an antigen with repeated units can crosslink the receptors, which is enough to activate the B cell without T cell cytokines.",
    "quote": "Each antibody on the B cell surface has two binding sites, and the repeated nature of T cell-independent antigen leads to crosslinking of the surface antibodies on the B cell. The crosslinking is enough to activate it in the absence of T cell cytokines.",
    "source": {
     "section": "21.4 The Adaptive Immune Response: B-lymphocytes and Antibodies",
     "url": "https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/21-4-the-adaptive-immune-response-b-lymphocytes-and-antibodies",
     "license": "CC BY 4.0",
     "citation": "Betts JG, Young KA, Wise JA, Johnson E, Poe B, Kruse DH, Korol O, Johnson JE, Womble M, DeSaix P (2022). Anatomy and Physiology 2e. OpenStax.",
     "access": "Access for free at https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/1-introduction"
    }
   },
   {
    "field": "function",
    "value": "It internalizes antigen caught by its surface antibody and presents processed pieces on class II MHC to Th2 helper cells, whose cytokines then complete its activation.",
    "quote": "Some of this antigen is internalized, processed, and presented to the Th2 cells on a class II MHC molecule. The T cell then binds using its antigen receptor and is activated to secrete cytokines that diffuse to the B cell, finally activating it completely.",
    "source": {
     "section": "21.4 The Adaptive Immune Response: B-lymphocytes and Antibodies",
     "url": "https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/21-4-the-adaptive-immune-response-b-lymphocytes-and-antibodies",
     "license": "CC BY 4.0",
     "citation": "Betts JG, Young KA, Wise JA, Johnson E, Poe B, Kruse DH, Korol O, Johnson JE, Womble M, DeSaix P (2022). Anatomy and Physiology 2e. OpenStax.",
     "access": "Access for free at https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/1-introduction"
    }
   },
   {
    "field": "function",
    "value": "All the receptors on one B cell are identical, but different B cells carry different recognition domains, so the population as a whole can bind a very wide range of molecular structures.",
    "quote": "The receptors of any particular B cell, as shown in are all the same, but the hundreds of millions of different B cells in an individual have distinct recognition domains that contribute to extensive diversity in the types of molecular structures to which they can bind.",
    "source": {
     "section": "42.2 Adaptive Immune Response",
     "url": "https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/42-2-adaptive-immune-response",
     "license": "CC BY 4.0",
     "citation": "Clark MA, Douglas M, Choi J (2018). Biology 2e. OpenStax.",
     "access": "Access for free at https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/1-introduction"
    }
   }
  ]
 },
